 ## Creating an admin user
 
-To create an admin user use the command [register_new_matrix_user](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/matrix-synapse/register_new_matrix_user.1.en.html) within the `compose-synapse` Docker container. Example:
+Run these commands only on a dComms host you administer, after Synapse has
+started. From the installation directory, set `DWEB_DOMAIN` to the same domain
+used by `run.sh` (replace the example below).
 
+```bash
+export DWEB_DOMAIN=server1.example.org
+docker compose \
+  -f ./conf/compose/docker-compose.yml \
+  -f ./conf/compose/element.docker-compose.yml \
+  -f ./conf/compose/synapse.docker-compose.yml \
+  exec synapse register_new_matrix_user \
+  -u dcomms-admin --admin -c /data/homeserver.yaml http://127.0.0.1:8008
 ```
-register_new_matrix_user -u dcomms-admin --admin -c /data/homeserver.yaml
-```
+
+The Compose service is `synapse`; its generated container name can vary.
+The command prompts for the password and confirmation. Avoid supplying a
+password with `-p`, which puts it in shell history and process arguments.
+
+This requires `registration_shared_secret` or
+`registration_shared_secret_path` in the loaded homeserver configuration.
+If neither exists, stop and follow the
+[shared-secret configuration guidance](https://element-hq.github.io/synapse/latest/usage/configuration/config_documentation.html#registration_shared_secret).
+The shared secret can create administrator accounts; do not publish it or the
+generated `homeserver.yaml`. You do not need to disable token-gated registration
+to bootstrap an administrator.
 
 ## User registration and registration tokens
 
-By default, Synapse is configured to require registration tokens to sign up. Synapse servers with open registrations are strongly discouraged, as they can result in severe spam and abuse. To generate a registration token you can follow the official API docs to [API access token](https://element-hq.github.io/synapse/latest/usage/administration/admin_api/index.html#making-an-admin-api-request), and then also to [generate a registration token](https://element-hq.github.io/synapse/latest/usage/administration/admin_api/registration_tokens.html). Example from cli of the dComms host:
+The installer sets `enable_registration: true` and
+`registration_requires_token: true`. Keep the token requirement enabled to
+limit unsolicited sign-ups.
 
-```curl
-user@dcomms-demo:~/dcomms-docker-compose# curl -X POST --header "Authorization: Bearer syt_abc123" http://localhost:8008/_synapse/admin/v1/registration_tokens/new -d '{}'
-{"token":"abc123","uses_allowed":null,"pending":0,"completed":0,"expiry_time":null}
+A **server administrator access token** authenticates an Admin API request.
+A **registration token** is an invitation for a new user. Give users only the
+registration token, never the administrator access token or shared secret.
+Obtain the administrator access token using your client's settings as described
+in the [Admin API documentation](https://element-hq.github.io/synapse/latest/usage/administration/admin_api/index.html).
+Keep the Admin API private and use the host's loopback listener.
+
+The following Bash example uses GNU `date`, already typical on Linux hosts.
+It creates one invitation valid for at most 24 hours. It prompts for the admin
+access token without putting it in shell history or curl's process arguments.
+Run it in an interactive terminal without session recording.
+
+```bash
+(
+  set +x
+  read -r -s -p "Server administrator access token: " SYNAPSE_ADMIN_TOKEN
+  printf '\n'
+  # Accept URL-safe token characters; reject curl-config control characters.
+  case "$SYNAPSE_ADMIN_TOKEN" in
+    ''|*[!A-Za-z0-9._~-]*) printf 'Unsupported token format.\n' >&2; exit 1 ;;
+  esac
+  expiry_ms=$(date -u -d '+24 hours' +%s000) || exit 1
+  printf 'header = "Authorization: Bearer %s"\n' "$SYNAPSE_ADMIN_TOKEN" |
+    curl -q --config - --noproxy '*' --proto '=http' \
+      --fail --silent --show-error --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 15 \
+      --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+      --data "{\"uses_allowed\":1,\"expiry_time\":$expiry_ms}" \
+      http://127.0.0.1:8008/_synapse/admin/v1/registration_tokens/new
+)
 ```
 
-Once you have generated a registration token, this can be handed out to users and they can sign-up.
+The response contains the invitation token; share it privately with the intended
+user. Check `uses_allowed` is `1` and `expiry_time` is a future timestamp in
+milliseconds. An empty JSON body (`{}`) instead creates an invitation with
+unlimited uses and no expiry. The subshell discards the entered admin token on
+exit; curl does not follow redirects.
+
+Use the [registration-token API](https://element-hq.github.io/synapse/latest/usage/administration/admin_api/registration_tokens.html)
+to list invitations, inspect pending/completed uses, and revoke an invitation
+with `DELETE /_synapse/admin/v1/registration_tokens/<token>` if it is disclosed
+or no longer needed. Expiry or deletion prevents future registrations; it does
+not remove accounts already created.
+
+These instructions cover Synapse-managed registration. If Matrix Authentication
+Service integration is enabled, use the MAS administration tools instead.
 
